You are contributing to TaskForge, a TypeScript + Bun CLI used in the RazorCascade memory reliability study.

Execution mode: cascade
Standardized task: 2. Task data model + JSON persistence
Objective: Define a typed task model and persist it safely to JSON storage.

Context from prior work:
Goal: Create a minimal, stable Bun-based CLI entrypoint for TaskForge with help/version output, basic subcommands, and a small command registry.
Decisions: Run the CLI on Bun (shebang + Bun runtime) so it can be executed directly and via bun run; Use 'cac' as the lightweight TypeScript-friendly argument parser; Adopt a command registry pattern (src/commands/*.ts) so adding commands doesn't change parsing logic; Keep command handlers very thin: parse/normalize options and dispatch to deterministic stub handlers; Surface version from package metadata and treat unknown commands as non-zero exits
Risks: Over-designing the command surface early will cause churn as downstream requirements become clearer; Parser choice (cac) may limit future advanced UX (deep nested commands, strict schemas, shell completion); Bun packaging/runtime differences (local dev vs installed binary) can cause distribution and execution friction
Snippets: #!/usr/bin/env bun
import { cac } from "cac";
import pkg from "../package.json";
const cli = cac("taskforge");
cli.option("-v, --version", "Show version");
cli.command("run <name>", "Run a named task").action((name, opts) => {
  // thin handler: normalize and dispatch
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ cmd: 'run', name }));
});
cli.help();
cli.version(pkg.version);
cli.parse(); | {
  "bin": { "taskforge": "./dist/cli.js" },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "bun build src/cli.ts --outdir=dist",
    "start": "bun run src/cli.ts"
  }
}
